ed they were  contaminated  the cdc took more than a month to  correct the issue and that really  hindered efforts by the public health  officials to limit the early  spread of coronavirus in the united  states  so when the fda reported their findings  there were  multiple things that went wrong the  negative control was contaminated for  the coronavirus test  they also noted that other countries had  created a single test for a single  target  whereas the united states of the cdc  created a single test but they had  multiple targets  they didn't just target the sars  cove ii they also targeted other sars  viruses  and by making the test so complex i  think it contributed  to the failure of the test it was  interesting that there was no final qc  before the test were shipped  because if anyone did use the test they  would have known right away that the  negative control would have failed  so you know in a limb system you can set  up limits like pass fail if it fails  then it can't be released so there's a  lot of automation  that either wasn't used or people  weren't trained in the proper use of  also the fda rules initially prevented  the state and commercial labs from  developing their own coronavirus test  kit  so even if they could develop it and had  the expertise they weren't able to  so this also delayed testing because the  cdc was the only shot  at creating a test and then the  cdc and the fda reversed course and they  lifted that rule at the end of february  so that other  commercial as well as university  laboratories were allowed to create the  coronavirus test kit as well  so there were a lot of things that came  into play here but  they were able to understand what went  wrong and work with private companies to  create  viable test kits next we're going to  look at  a case study with the canadian  government how they had rejected not  only nasal swabs but also  coronavirus testing kits when testing is  done on reagents and supplies in the  laboratory  