vid test kit  then we'll look at the rejection of  sampling kits by the canadian government  and finally we'll look at challenges  with vaccine development  the u.s government via the cdc was  tasked to  create and distribute covet testing kits  to all the health departments throughout  the united states  headlines soon appeared that the cdc had  botched the coronavirus testing  further investigation found that the  test kits that were sent to hundreds of  state and local  public health departments across the  united states were contaminated so their  negative control was actually coming up  positive and many  public health labs didn't realize the  test wasn't working properly and they  were reporting false positives  and as you can imagine this led not only  to quite a bit of  confusion early on but it was very  difficult to do  tracing of positive patients in terms of  who they were in contact with the  assisting containing the outbreak  because some of the  positives weren't really positives so as  you can imagine  the fda came in to investigate the cdc  and they had a number of questions for  the cdc team  and what they found was not only there  was a  contamination of their negative control  but they also found that two of the  three cdc labs that were located in  atlanta  didn't follow their own manufacturing  guidelines they didn't follow the  standard operating procedures that  should have been linked in their limbs  to the manufacturing of test kits  they found that there was a huge lack of  expertise and  and training uh the positive and  negative controls were made in the same  room  and for anyone familiar with pcr when  you're performing pcr you're  amplificating  nucleic acid and during that  amplification  there's nucleic acid in the air so it's  very important to change  clothing there was no changing of the  lab coat so the people that were  performing the pcr were also the people  that were making the negative controls  and that's how they determined they were  contaminated  the cdc took more than a month to  correct the issue and that really  hindered efforts by the public health  officials to limit the early  spread of coronavirus in the united  states  so when the fda reported their findings  there were  multiple things that went wrong the  negative control was contaminated for  the coronavirus test  they also noted that other countries had  created a single test for a single  target  whereas the united states of the cdc  created a single test but they had  multiple targets  they didn't just target the sars  cove ii they also targeted other sars  viruses  and by making the test so complex i  think it contributed  to the failure of the test it was  interesting that there was no final qc  before the test were shipped  because if anyone did use the test they  would have known right away that the  negative control would have failed  so you know in a limb system you can set  up limits like pass fail if it fails  then it can't be released so there's a  lot of automation  that either wasn't used or people  weren't trained in the proper use of  also the fda rules initially prevented  the state and commercial labs from  developing their own coronavirus test  kit  so even if they could develop it and had  the expertise they weren't able to  so this also delayed testing because the  cdc was the only shot  at creating a test and then the  cdc and the fda reversed course and they  lifted that rule at the end of february  so that other  commercial as well as university  laboratories were allowed to create the  coronavirus test kit as well  so there were a lot of things that came  into play here but  they were able to understand what went  wrong and work with private companies to  create  viable test kits next we're going to  look at  a case study with the canadian  government how they had rejected not  only nasal swabs but also  coronavirus testing kits when testing is  done on reagents and supplies in the  laboratory  oftentimes the vendor will ship with  those reagents what's called a c of a  a certificate of analysis so in the case  of  nasal swabs or petri plates typically  the c of a will state that the  item was tested and found to be sterile  so that means that when they put it on  microbiological media  nothing was cultured nothing grew the  ontario government received over a  hundred thousand contaminated unusable  swabs for covet testing  how did they know this they basically  didn't  they did they want to verify the  manufacturer's  certificate of analysis and they did  their own testing and when they  um incubated covid testing swabs they  found out that they were contaminated  with what they believed was mold so  there were  over a hundred thousand contaminated  swabs that were unused  early on in the pandemic when they were  trying to identify  positive patients a second example from  the canadian government  is uh more than 6400 test kits were  contaminated  prior to being used thankfully they  found that  they were contaminated with the bacteria  the previous one was contaminated with  the mold  and if you realize that these  nasopharyngeal test kits  are meant to basically go into someone's  sinuses and if they're contaminated you  could be inoculating people with mold or  bacteria  it's a very scary proposition so it's  really um  wonderful that they determined that they  were contaminated and did not use them  but i often wonder how many cases where  people were in such a hurry to  screen people so that they could contain  the outbreak and they didn't do testing  on the materials that they received too  often people believe the manufacturers  certificate of analysis they have a lot  of faith in that  and other organizations often do some  form of lot testing so they may not test  every single sample  but they'll test you know one percent or  five percent  to make sure that the c of a is accurate  so if we look at the coronavirus  pandemic it really was the perfect storm  there were so many factors involved  early on  the data that the government presented  to  the people was that mass don't work and  it came out that it  wasn't that mass didn't work they didn't  want people to purchase masks because  there weren't enough masks for the  healthcare workers  also only the cdc could make the kova  test kit and they picked their primers  and probes  to target even though other countries  had already created test kits so there  really is no standardized test kit  everyone's using different test kits  that they've created within their  country  multiplex versus a single test the cdc  made a much more complex  coronavirus test initially and that  later they moved to a single target  single test configuration  other negative controls were  contaminated we've also found out that  some of the laboratories ran the cycles  too long so that everything would come  up positive  so with all the false positives out  there it was really difficult to  understand  the true picture of the the outbreak in  the country  there was a shortage of ppe a lot of it  was coming from  china there were quality issues  hospitals were overwhelmed  in many cases in italy where people were  on life support they were making  decisions based on the age of the  patient so it was a really horrible time  later masks do work where to if it makes  you feel safe so  there was so much misinformation  confusing information being  provided then finally we write about  contaminated vaccines where  millions of doses were disposed of and  all the while there were cases cases  cases clusters  of the outbreak continuing and then just  boom  full-blown pandemic  our final case study is looking at  manufacturing issues  vaccine development challenges by  pharmaceutical companies  all related to product quality there's a  manufacturing  facility called emergent they were  located in baltimore they were actually  making the vaccines for both  johnson and johnson as well as  astrazeneca  as you can imagine the fda  began to investigate once again and they  found that the facility in baltimore had  numerous  challenges that they were dealing with  the vaccines were released to ship  but no one really could attest to the  quality of the vaccines they were sent  to canada and mexico  astrazeneca's vaccine that was made at  the same facility as the johnson and  johnson vaccine was made  they discarded millions of doses and  they said they  because they were potentially  contaminated i don't know if any data is  going to be available because the cdc or  the fda  is still investigating but they had over  15 million doses  that were discarded because of  contamination what the fda did report  is that there were problems with  disinfection practices  the size and design of the facility the  way in which the workers were handling  the raw materials  and also training of the workers  so if we look at the impact of having a  culture  of poor data integrity you can see that  bad data can cause a lot of problems  the most important is loss of life in  the first few months of a pandemic  they're critical in containing an  outbreak  and there was an opportunity lost with  the defective test kits  the financial loss in the billions i'm  sure maybe even higher  political outfall the reputation loss of  trust of the  testing laboratories of the  manufacturers that are creating  not only the test kits but the  nasopharyngeal swabs  but the vaccine so there's a lot at  stake to get it right  and when you embrace a a  culture of data integrity things go  right much more often than in companies  that  don't embrace data integrity  now because many of the investigations  by the fda are ongoing  we don't have a final report of what  happened in terms of details with the  coronavirus test kit that was botched or  the vaccines  or the nasopharyngeal swabs but what we  can do is look at examples of integrity  issues from past  fda audits they found that  analysts in the laboratory save files to  a personal computer not on a network  they found that users were sharing  passwords because they didn't want to  pay for software licenses to basically  save  cost they often find that the audit  trail is disabled  so that people can change results  without providing a reason or  explanation  a lack of training for laboratory staff  we saw that as an initial finding  by the fda in the creation of the covet  kits at the cdc  they've also detected hplc integration  parameters that were changed and re-ran  until results passed in other labs  quality tests were not performed i think  it's clear to  say this is what happened at the cdc no  one tested the test kits before  they were sent to hundreds of  laboratories across the united states  the standard operating procedures were  not followed that was another finding of  the fda of their initial investigation  of the coronavirus test kit manufacturer  at the cdc  unsanctioned sample testing with labels  such as demo test  blank or trial inadequate access control  so when you  have a limbs you can basically give  analysts different roles within the  limbs and you can assign them privileges  so  an analyst can maybe enter their results  and validate their results but they're  unable to approve them that would be a  supervisor  and that's just an example of having  peer control or peer review  in your laboratory so those were not  configured or were  configured improperly users were logged  in to a shared account so that they  could change data without  understanding who actually changed the  data so there are a number of ways that  people can  get around controls that are put in  place to have good quality solution  now if we look at a few methods that  which support data integrity  one under best practices is to execute a  risk-based validation  so what is this it basically takes  validation and puts it into three  categories say  hi where the failure would severely  affect safety or quality process  medium a failure would have a moderate  impact on the safety and quality  processes  or a low where a failure would have a  minor impact on  patient safety or product quality so by  using this matrix you can basically  define steps in your process that  require  more attention versus those that may not  be as critical also ensure that partners  and vendors are iso certified this  basically means that their regular  regularly audited by a external party  to ensure that their quality management  system is adhering to the guidelines of  the international standards organization  so you know that that vendor has a  quality system in place versus  a vendor that you found on the internet  and has no quality system and no  certifications  look at the audit trail reports of the  limbs how often are results changed is  it something that happens  very frequently or does it happen not at  all  there's a lot of information to be had  in the audit trail report  i'm looking at the change control  process does the laboratory have one  how does it work are the end users  trained in this process  quality i t and validate systems how is  the  what role does the information  technology team play  with the limbs how do they help the  laboratory do they have documentation  that systems have been validated that  calculations  are accurate and concise do they have  documentation on instrument integration  where is that data going from the  instrument  in the limbs what fields is that data  being placed into  does the organization have a business  continuity plan so if they lose power  or if their all their data becomes  corrupt where's the backup located is it  off site  is it how long would it take to get that  database up and running again i'm also  providing data integrity training to  their analysts  is this something that is in the the  organization's dna or is this something  that people  are not even familiar with and then also  also looking at  how the data is archived and also  checking those  archives of those backups because it's  one thing to say yes we back up our data  you know twice a day or or every five  hours but it's another to take a look at  that to make sure that the backups  are backing up correctly  so in summary bad data is not only  dangerous  dangerous it can be deadly as we've just  seen automation enhances data quality in  that there's no  opportunity for transcription errors it  can occur in the background in the case  of sensors and i'll be recorded  and only provide alerts when there's a  problem  eliminating transcription errors is huge  because you know you have a higher  quality of data  and you don't waste valuable resources  re-entering results from an instrument  into a limbs  also by having sensors that are  calibrated to the nest standards your  you know that your  have calibrated equipment that's taking  measurements and you are  in compliance so when you have your  regulatory audits you have the data to  to back that up there's also an audit  trail so that any changes  made within the database especially to  any results are  documented you've tracked the who what  when why  you have date and time stamps of when  changes to data were made  by whom and what the reason for the  change was and that there's also data  security  to ensure that no one can go in and  alter data  so that you basically have a username  and password the system is  21cfr part 11 compliant with electronic  signatures  and also the cost of bad data can not  only shut down a business and hurt a  company's reputation  but it can have lasting consequences far  beyond a bad audit  it can lead to death as we've seen in  this  current pandemic now i don't want to end  the presentation on a negative note  but i do want people to realize the  importance of data integrity and data  quality  it really does matter if anyone's  interested in learning more or  would like a copy of the presentation  please feel free to reach out thank you  so much for your time have a wonderful  rest of your  day or evening or morning thank you


